"A food caterer had to whip up gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most folks would stick to just one hotdog, he made enough for half the guests to grab two. But then, 40 guests turned up, and everyone was keen on a second hotdog. How many guests missed out on a second hotdog?","A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?",26,26,26,26,"Step 1: Determine how many hotdogs were prepared.
- There were 36 guests expected.
- Enough were made so that half the guests could have two hotdogs.
- That means: half (18 guests) get 2 each → 18 × 2 = 36 hotdogs
- The other half (18 guests) get 1 each → 18 × 1 = 18 hotdogs
- Total prepared = 36 + 18 = 54 hotdogs

Step 2: Determine how many hotdogs were needed when 40 guests showed up and all wanted two.
- Each of the 40 guests wants 2 hotdogs → 40 × 2 = 80 hotdogs needed

Step 3: Calculate the shortfall in second hotdogs.
- First, everyone can get at least one hotdog as long as total hotdogs ≥ 40. We have 54, so after giving 1 each to 40 guests, 54 − 40 = 14 hotdogs remain for second servings.
- Number of guests who get a second hotdog = 14
- Number of guests who miss out on a second hotdog = 40 − 14 = 26

#### 26",,"Step 1: Determine how many hotdogs were prepared.
- There were 36 guests expected.
- Enough were made so that half the guests could have two hotdogs.
- That means: half (18 guests) get 2 each → 18 × 2 = 36 hotdogs
- The other half (18 guests) get 1 each → 18 × 1 = 18 hotdogs
- Total prepared = 36 + 18 = 54 hotdogs
Step 2: Determine how many hotdogs were needed when 40 guests showed up and all wanted two.
- Each of the 40 guests wants 2 hotdogs → 40 × 2 = 80 hotdogs needed
Step 3: Calculate the shortfall in second hotdogs.
- First, everyone can get at least one hotdog as long as total hotdogs ≥ 40. We have 54, so after giving 1 each to 40 guests, 54 − 40 = 14 hotdogs remain for second servings.
- Number of guests who get a second hotdog = 14
- Number of guests who miss out on a second hotdog = 40 − 14 = 26
#### 26","Let’s define what the caterer prepared and what was needed.

- Original guest count expected: 36
- He prepared enough so that half the guests could have two hotdogs.
- That means: 18 guests get 2 hotdogs, and the other 18 get 1 hotdog.
- Total hotdogs prepared = (18 × 2) + (18 × 1) = 36 + 18 = 54

But actually:
- 40 guests showed up.
- Everyone wanted a second hotdog, so total hotdogs needed = 40 × 2 = 80

Shortfall in hotdogs = 80 − 54 = 26

Each missing hotdog corresponds to one guest not getting their second hotdog. So, the number of guests who did not get a second hotdog is 26.

