"James digs swimming and has to cross a 20-mile lake. He swims at 2 miles per hour. He covers 60% of the lake, then chills on an island for half the time he spent swimming. After that, he finishes up the rest of the way at half his usual speed. How long did it take him to get across?","James loves to go swimming and has to swim across a 20-mile lake. He can swim at a pace of 2 miles per hour. He swims 60% of the distance. After that, he stops on an island and rests for half as long as the swimming time. He then finishes the remaining distance while going half the speed. How long did it take him to get across the lake?",17,17,17,17,"Step 1: Determine distance swum before the break.
- James covers 60% of the 20-mile lake.
- Distance = 0.60 × 20 = 12 miles.

Step 2: Time for the first part.
- Speed = 2 mph.
- Time = distance/speed = 12 / 2 = 6 hours.

Step 3: Rest time on the island.
- He rests for half the time he spent swimming initially.
- Rest time = 0.5 × 6 = 3 hours.

Step 4: Remaining distance.
- Total distance = 20 miles, already swum 12 miles.
- Remaining distance = 20 − 12 = 8 miles.

Step 5: Speed for the remaining part.
- He swims at half his usual speed after the break.
- New speed = 2 / 2 = 1 mph.

Step 6: Time for the remaining part.
- Time = distance/speed = 8 / 1 = 8 hours.

Step 7: Total time.
- Total time = first swim + rest + second swim = 6 + 3 + 8 = 17 hours.

