"John uses a driving service to get to work every day. His job is 30 miles away, so he travels there and back daily. He works 5 days a week for 50 weeks each year. They charge him $2 for every mile, and he also tips his driver an extra $150 each month. How much does he end up paying for the rides each year?",John hires a driving service to get him to work each day. His work is 30 miles away and he has to go there and back each day. He goes to work 5 days a week for 50 weeks a year. He gets charged $2 per mile driven and he also gives his driver a $150 bonus per month. How much does he pay a year for driving?,31800,31800,318,318,"Let’s break it down:

- Daily distance: 30 miles to work + 30 miles back = 60 miles per day.
- Days per year: 5 days/week × 50 weeks/year = 250 days.
- Total miles per year: 60 miles/day × 250 days = 15,000 miles.
- Cost per mile: $2 → Ride cost per year: 15,000 miles × $2/mile = $30,000.

Now add tips:
- Tip per month: $150.
- Months per year: 12 → Annual tips: 150 × 12 = $1,800.

Total annual cost = Ride cost + Tips = $30,000 + $1,800 = $31,800.

